WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.850
Film Courage: What do you love about being
a director?

00:00:01.850 --> 00:00:05.630
Tom Oesch, Writer/Director of DANGER ONE Movie:
Good question.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:21.529
I mean this might sound cheesy and I’ve
said this before but it kind 

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:29.970
of goes back to the experience of me being
13 and seeing something so awe-inspiring as

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.930
JURASSIC PARK.

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:39.630
And again if that film hadn’t been made,
my life would have been completely different.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:46.200
Like if I hadn’t seen that movie…that
Saturday afternoon put me on a very specific

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.200
path in life.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.580
If that hadn’t happened I would not have
moved half-way across the world, I wouldn’t

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:58.730
have gone to USC, I wouldn’t have met hundreds
of interesting people here in in LA, I wouldn’t

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:04.270
be speaking English 24/7 (I would be speaking
German), and I would have never made this

00:01:04.270 --> 00:01:05.270
movie.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:08.960
As a matter of fact none of these people that
I met on this movie would have ever met each

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:09.960
other.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:13.540
It’s just insane how it snowballs just because
someone made a movie, It’s insane how this

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:14.540
just snowballs because someone made a movie.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.470
And I’m not saying I’m going to make JURASSIC
PARK (because that’s absurd) because it’s

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.460
so good.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:28.890
But the idea…and maybe that’s just me
being sentimental…the idea that a movie,

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:36.270
that some day, some where in this world, some
kid might see the thing you’ve made, and

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:42.850
respond to it in a certain way for whatever
reason and then decide to pursue a certain

00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:48.190
path in life, that’s pretty wild you know?

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:54.060
To affect someone’s life like that with
something like a movie, we’re not doing

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:55.080
brain surgery.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.799
And again maybe this will never happen, I’m
just me being sentimental, but I like that

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:04.880
this could happen.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:13.170
And I think I said this before, just because
my life is very structured and organized on

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:25.380
normal days, there’s a routine 
to direct and to be on set.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:32.371
It yanks you out of that routine and you kind
of just get thrown in the deep end of the

00:02:32.371 --> 00:02:35.540
pool and it’s exciting, it’s thrilling.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.780
And also because so many things could go wrong.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:46.990
It just kind of feels that you’re parachuting
out of plane (it’s a little adventure) and

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:52.120
even on this film (like on any production)
there were moments where there was a lot of

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.450
tension and people bumped heads but I would
always tell them like “Guys, we could be

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:02.800
sitting in an office right now doing nine-to-five
and then go home.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.910
We’re in Vernon crawling around in this
creepy neighborhood telling story.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.580
How amazing is that?”

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:16.739
Oftentimes it will click with people and they’ll
be like “Yeah, you’re right.”

00:03:16.739 --> 00:03:18.100
And every day is so different.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.020
There is no day that is just like the other
one because you’re at a new location, there

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.120
are new actors, and that’s just an amazing
job.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:34.340
And I don’t even look at it as a job, you
just feel very alive.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.270
You feel like “Oh, my God!”

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.440
Now you’re living life because it’s so
the opposite of routine.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:44.849
Film Courage: And is it too the sense of possibility
that everything could totally fail?

00:03:44.849 --> 00:03:47.500
And there is that challenge of trying to keep
it from not failing…it’s almost like a

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.590
game?

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:55.040
Tom: Yes, and when I say you almost feel alive
maybe that’s just the anxiety of “Oh my

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.730
God everything could go wrong.”

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.310
But you just feel very awake.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:12.220
I mean you obviously don’t want it to go
wrong but I look at it as a little adventure.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:20.530
Film Courage: So then on the flipside, what
do you dislike about being a director?: I

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:27.419
mean I guess it’s…and we’ve addressed
it before…it’s the waiting game.

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:32.860
It’s the stuff between the directing where
it’s just like you’re getting your stuff

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:38.589
out there, you’re hoping it’s going to
click with someone and it’s a lot of waiting

00:04:38.589 --> 00:04:49.999
before people take weeks to get back to you
so once the ball gets rolling it’s the most

00:04:49.999 --> 00:04:50.999
awesome thing.

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:53.990
It’s just the in between, you know.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:05:02.369
Because especially as freelancers you’re
always (and I know a lot of actors and editors,

00:05:02.369 --> 00:05:07.319
they all feel the same way), you think you’re
never going to work again every time you’re

00:05:07.319 --> 00:05:08.319
not working.

00:05:08.319 --> 00:05:09.819
You’re like “This is the last time.

00:05:09.819 --> 00:05:11.680
I’m never going to get another job.”

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.909
So the downtime is no fun.

00:05:15.909 --> 00:05:22.439
But you try to fill that time with creating
new stuff, getting things ready so when you

00:05:22.439 --> 00:05:26.300
meet that next person that wants to make something,
you have things to show.

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:31.470
Film Courage: I’ve heard some people saying
they like that not knowing in-between because

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:37.309
you know because if someone has had a 9-to-5
job, it’s like an endless just calendar

00:05:37.309 --> 00:05:42.069
full of…you know exactly what’s going
to happen, pointless meetings and meetings

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:43.069
about meetings.

00:05:43.069 --> 00:05:45.770
Tom: Oh, you mean when you have a 9-to-5 job,
the downtime then?

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.139
Film Courage: No, when you don’t have that.

00:05:49.139 --> 00:05:53.330
Some people enjoy that not knowing, there
is something very thrilling about…even though

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:57.219
it’s scary there is something…so you’re
saying like a high…

00:05:57.219 --> 00:05:58.860
Tom: It’s similar guess then.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:02.120
Film Courage: Yes, it’s similar because
there’s a sense of possibility, there’s

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:04.879
a sense of the unknown, I don’t know?

00:06:04.879 --> 00:06:11.520
Tom: That’s true but when you’re actually
making it and you’re on set there is forward

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:15.622
movement and when there’s downtime (even
when you’re making stuff) there’s not

00:06:15.622 --> 00:06:16.622
necessarily any of that (you know what I mean)
because when there’s downtime and you’re

00:06:16.622 --> 00:06:21.840
making stuff there is not necessarily any
of that because you might be down for two

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.840
months.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:27.400
And at the end of those two months you’re
at the same place that you were before.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:32.259
Once you get on a set and you’re actually
making a movie, two months later, you have

00:06:32.259 --> 00:06:39.490
something, something got made so it just feels
like there is some forward momentum, things

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.020
are happening, things are being made.

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.669
I like being productive.

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:51.429
Film Courage: At the end of the 23 day shoot
when you wrapped when you were driving home

00:06:51.429 --> 00:06:55.970
(from the whole 10 minutes from set or from
wherever you were) how did that feel?

00:06:55.970 --> 00:07:02.819
Tom: It’s funny because I talk about this…it’s
really weird?

00:07:02.819 --> 00:07:10.659
Just because like…it was actually an emotional
drive home because it’s the first time in

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:16.289
two months that things slowed down for me
to be “Oh my God, did this just happen?”

00:07:16.289 --> 00:07:25.389
You know how life is like a series of milestones?

00:07:25.389 --> 00:07:28.990
For me, moving to LA was one of them.

00:07:28.990 --> 00:07:35.749
For other people getting married, having children,
there are a handful of these milestones.

00:07:35.749 --> 00:07:47.849
For me since I was 15 or 13 this had been
my goal and oftentimes I was obsessing over

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:48.849
it.

00:07:48.849 --> 00:07:56.169
I probably sacrificed a lot just to focus
on this.

00:07:56.169 --> 00:07:59.240
And then for it to…what was it?

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:07.800
Probably 20 years later for that to actually
happen, it was like this weird feeling of

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:08.800
calm.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.959
Because all of the sudden everything kind
of…while you have 20 years of your eye on

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:14.959
the prize..

00:08:14.959 --> 00:08:18.929
Every day you’re working on that goal of
getting a film made.

00:08:18.929 --> 00:08:24.479
Then all of the sudden that film has been
made and you’re like “Oh my God?

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:25.479
Now what?”

00:08:25.479 --> 00:08:31.409
On an emotional level it felt like life could
now go into a million different ways.

00:08:31.409 --> 00:08:33.789
Now that I’ve done this do I actually want
to make another movie?

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:36.550
What am I going to do next?

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:39.900
Before that it was “Make a movie, make a
movie, you’ve got to make a movie.”

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:52.940
Now you’ve made one and it’s like…it
was almost liberating in a really weird way.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:54.030
But then you realize “Oh my God?

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:56.610
I actually have to finish this movie.”

00:08:56.610 --> 00:09:04.890
Then you’ve obviously learned a lot of things
and you want to apply all the lessons I learned

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.060
on this movie by making another movie and
do better next time.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:09.060
But then BOOM!

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:17.970
You’re back on that straight arrow trying
to make something else.

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:25.380
But you’re like (what do they call it) the
eye of the storm for a little while and everything

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.760
just came to a standstill and that was me
in the car driving home from Griffith Park

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:34.830
in LA (because that was the last night we
shot was in Griffith Park) was driving back

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:42.660
from Griffith Park to Downtown where I kind
of realized “Oh my God?

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.840
The thing I set out to do when I was 13 just
happened.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.300
Because when you’re making a movie, you’re
not thinking of nothing like that, you’re

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:53.350
just trying to keep up.

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:54.790
Film Courage: That’s pretty cool.

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:55.970
Were you by yourself?

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:56.970
Tom: Yes.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.800
Film Courage: Was it late at night?

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:00.900
Tom: No, actually the sun was coming up.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:01.990
It was perfect.

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:10.500
Because I was actually driving through Griffith
Park with the sun coming up behind the hills.

